            <ArticleTitle>Greater occipital nerve blockade in cervicogenic headache.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>720-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Cervicocogenic headache (CeH) is a relatively common disorder. Although on ideal treatment is available so far, blockades in different structures and nerves may be temporarily effective. We studied the effects of 1-2 mL 0.5% bupivacaine injection at the ipsilateral greater occipital nerve (GON) in 41 CeH patients. The pain is significantly reduced both immediately and as long as 7 days after the blockade. The improvement is less marked during the first two days, a phenomenon we called &quot;tilde pattern&quot;. GON blockades may reduce the pool of exaggerated sensory input and antagonize a putative &quot;wind-up-like effect&quot; which may explain the headache improvement.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
